<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="scales-modes-and-chords" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Scales, Modes and Chords</title>

  


  <introduction>
    <p>
      The following shows that Chords, Scales and Modes are actually one and the same collection of notes. Scales have the notes arranged in a horizontal linear fashion and are the notes you could use with the particular chord during improvisation. Chord notes from the scale (diatonic) are arranged vertically and are played simultaneously.  
    </p>
  </introduction>

  
   <p> A scale or mode is a subset of the 12 notes of an octave, and has two components:</p>
   <ol>
    <li>The notes they are comprised of;</li>
    <li>A tonal centre (or root note).</li>
    </ol>
 
  <p>The (diatonic) Major Scale has 7 notes, but scales can have fewer or more notes (all the way up to the Chromatic Scale with 12 notes).</p>
  <p>If you keep all the notes of a scale or mode the same but change the tonal centre, you create a new scale or mode. So, a mode is a scale created by establishing a new root note within a pre-existing scale. Modes are just a different way of thinking about scales and keys. Using modes allows us to allocate an individual mode/scale to every single chord in a progression.</p>
 <p>
Scales and chords are interrelated. A scale is a horizontal representation of a particular collection of notes and is built up in 2nds. A chord is a vertical representation of that same collection of notes and is built up in 3rds.</p>
<p>For example, if we take all the white notes and go up in 2nds, we get the C major scale (C D E F G A B); if we take those same notes and go up in 3rds, we get CMaj13 (C E G B D F A) (the ♮11 is not an available tension, but ignore this for now).
</p>
<p>Similarly, taking all the white notes except B♭ and going up in 2nds from C gives C Mixolydian (C D E F G A B♭); taking the same notes in 3rds gives C13 (C E G B♭ D F A) (again, ignore the ♮11 for now).
Likewise, taking all the white notes except F# and going up in 2nds from C gives C Lydian (C D E F# G A B); and in 3rds, CMaj13#11 (C E G B D F# A). In summary:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>C major scale has the same notes as a CMaj13 chord;</li>
  <li>C Mixolydian scale has the same notes as a C13 chord;</li>
  <li>C Lydian scale has the same notes as a CMaj13#11 chord.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are the scales you would use to improvise over these chords.
</p>

